[[:db/retract 17592186080571 :answer-field/choices 17592186080572] [:db/retract 17592186080571 :answer-field/choices 17592186080573] {:db/id 17592186083199 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ac24346d-5ce4-50ce-a3b5-301fb4761c13"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083590 :question/problem "Given the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=6{a}_{n}-5{a}_{n-1},\\,{a}_{1}=1,\\,{a}_{2}=21,\\,n\\in N,\\,n\\ge 2\n$$\n\nuse strong induction to prove the following statement:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={5}^{n}-4,\\,∀n\\in N\n$$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base cases $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}={{field-5}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-8}} for $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-4$ for all {{field-9}} for some {{field-10}}. In particular, we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={{field-11}} \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: Using the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =({{field-13}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{a}_{k}-5{a}_{k-1} & =6({{field-14}})-5({{field-15}}) \\\\  & =5({{field-16}})-{{field-17}} \\\\  & ={{field-18}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of strong induction, ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-4$ is true for all {{field-19}}." :question/worked-solution "The principle of strong induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),$ $P(2),$ $P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true (as well as $P(2),P(3),P(4),…,$ if required), and\n\n- $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,{a}_{n}={5}^{n}-4\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base cases. Since the first application of the recurrence formula ${a}_{n+1}=6{a}_{n}-5{a}_{n-1}$ does not apply until ${a}_{3},$ we must check the base cases $P(1)$ and $P(2).$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove our statement is true for the base cases $n=1,2.$ \nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{5}^{1}-4=1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for $n=2,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=21,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{5}^{2}-4=21.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\nNext, we write down our (strong) **inductive hypothesis,** which is to assume that $P(n)$ is true *for all* $n\\le k,$ where $k\\ge 2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-4$ for all $n\\le k$ for some $k\\ge 2.$ In particular, we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={5}^{k}-4 \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={5}^{k-1}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the inductive step.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={5}^{k+1}-4.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nUsing the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =6{a}_{k}-5{a}_{k-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{a}_{k}-5{a}_{k-1} & =6({5}^{k}-4)-5({5}^{k-1}-4) \\\\  & =6({5}^{k})-6(4)-5({5}^{k-1})+5(4) \\\\  & =6({5}^{k})-24-{5}^{k}+20 \\\\  & =5({5}^{k})-4 \\\\  & ={5}^{k+1}-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, by the principle of strong induction, ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-4$ is true for all $n\\in N.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-795f7a56-1f53-5c66-9063-ab633fdfb841" "answer-field-5ec8e422-43be-5be6-a9a1-e4d7d4546167" "answer-field-06974d7c-5f2a-523e-82bf-3f22dc1f6af9" "answer-field-fb7c458b-b260-5f49-a82b-0e33e12c3e4c" "answer-field-87ad0cfc-4304-56fc-956d-672ca71b949e" "answer-field-04451360-7da2-572d-bca0-b7978e85d275" "answer-field-5c507b57-8d38-501c-8405-9e9db8044ae8" "answer-field-a130dd08-e157-5fd9-9952-a46107b1d57d" "answer-field-55b2615c-ae57-5947-8d87-3fdcd06f4b72" "answer-field-eb601b79-351a-52b0-b12a-40bd282111c0" "answer-field-0924a375-e5aa-514b-b423-bd118eed981d" "answer-field-5078e9df-26f4-5315-b5bd-6b0443d5a0c7" "answer-field-18fea9e6-4a2d-53d3-8c4b-e553f66a6a2c" "answer-field-bc24f97a-49f0-52fc-a22d-734527623253" "answer-field-47ab5f2a-7923-563b-b21f-f2d4aa7e7320" "answer-field-89a3e2a2-2f3b-5be3-92f2-04ec71a2ce9b" "answer-field-0273d17f-dfc2-51b3-aac3-7d79b47b0da3" "answer-field-68ec3c71-f18b-5f22-8e3e-a9e648a6948f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186080571 :answer-field/correct "answer-e87eff46-132c-5c56-9839-6568821027e4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e87eff46-132c-5c56-9839-6568821027e4" "answer-990507c8-8113-565b-a377-3722f691f05a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e87eff46-132c-5c56-9839-6568821027e4" :answer/id #uuid "e87eff46-132c-5c56-9839-6568821027e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1,2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-990507c8-8113-565b-a377-3722f691f05a" :answer/id #uuid "990507c8-8113-565b-a377-3722f691f05a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-795f7a56-1f53-5c66-9063-ab633fdfb841" :answer-field/id #uuid "795f7a56-1f53-5c66-9063-ab633fdfb841" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-fiel