-academy-id "q-183963" :question/problem "Given the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=4{a}_{n}-{3}^{n},\\,{a}_{1}=7,\\,n\\in N,\n$$\n\nuse mathematical induction to prove the following statement: \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={4}^{n}+{3}^{n},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side of our statement, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}={{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: Using the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & ={{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{k}-{3}^{k} & =4({{field-10}})-{3}^{k} \\\\  & ={4}^{k+1}+{{field-11}} \\\\  & ={{field-12}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${a}_{n}={4}^{n}+{3}^{n}$ is true for all {{field-13}}." :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),$ $P(2),$ $P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,{a}_{n}={4}^{n}+{3}^{n}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$ For the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=7,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{4}^{1}+{3}^{1}=7.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}={4}^{k}+{3}^{k}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the inductive step.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={4}^{k+1}+{3}^{k+1}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nUsing the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =4{a}_{k}-{3}^{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{k}-{3}^{k} & =4({4}^{k}+{3}^{k})-{3}^{k} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {4}^{k}+4\\cdot {3}^{k}-{3}^{k} \\\\  & ={4}^{k+1}+3\\cdot {3}^{k} \\\\  & ={4}^{k+1}+{3}^{k+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, by the principle of induction, ${a}_{n}={4}^{n}+{3}^{n}$ is true for all $n\\in N.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-04c8c8b3-b5f9-5127-98f2-c9b10fdcea86" "answer-field-7918dd1d-dae2-53ee-85e1-901c4a5859f1" "answer-field-5ce58305-13d3-52e1-a078-df5e171cc367" "answer-field-86a82a22-4122-5637-b0a0-4ad01a41f516" "answer-field-babbdbfa-808c-5071-8482-8ae0e3f5e3f3" "answer-field-cefbb136-6985-56fb-b68a-ed3c57903491" "answer-field-2ba013d1-2f74-5f96-985a-c375eee95d85" "answer-field-04554eb7-5cc8-5bc9-a21b-295be83f639f" "answer-field-0ab88a0b-b82b-5b5b-b588-0ef20127fbb2" "answer-field-9b256641-6ba4-54c3-88fe-ac096e8eefec" "answer-field-162a564d-41b0-5784-b67d-3224cc96d6ed" "answer-field-d7c858af-175b-5399-8a66-3bcb84e582ea" "answer-field-9b2ac3b3-4dd1-5b8e-83a6-64bd925b4a36"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-04c8c8b3-b5f9-5127-98f2-c9b10fdcea86" :answer-field/id #uuid "04c8c8b3-b5f9-5127-98f2-c